On Fri, Oct 15, 2010 at 12:23 AM, Jonathan Jones <eyeonus@gmail.com> wrote:
>>
>> > (1) jei = du'u xu kau
>>
>> > (2) x1 jei (bridi) kei x2 = x1 se jetlai lo du'u (bridi) kei x2
>
> It looks to me as though the distinction between 1) and 2) is, 1) can have a
> value of 0 or 1, whereas 2) can have any value between 0 and 1 inclusive.

No, that's not it.

In (1) "jei" is a du'u, it's the kind of thing you can use as the x2
of djuno, facki, jdice, senpi,  and so on. Also, it's fine to answer a
"xu" question with "je'u ru'e" or whatever method you want to use for
intermediate truth values. But in (1) jei is a proposition, not a
number.

In (2) it is a number, not a du'u, not something you want as the x2 of
djuno, facki, jdice, senpi, etc.

mu'o mi'e xorxes

